# Rank 1534 / CVE-2026-29181: OpenTelemetry Go's Baggage propagator
# accumulated members from every baggage header without any W3C limit, so a
# request carrying many headers (or huge ones) drove unbounded parsing and
# memory growth in every service using the propagator (resource exhaustion,
# CWE-400/CWE-770; PR #7880). extractMultiBaggage in propagation/baggage.go
# did `for _, bStr := range bVals { currBag, err := baggage.Parse(bStr);
# if err != nil { continue }; members = append(members,
# currBag.Members()...) }` -- nothing caps the accumulated member count or
# the combined bytes across headers. Pool commit aa1894e09 is the real fix
# (COMMIT_OK -- "Comply with W3C Baggage specification limits (#7880)"): the
# loop breaks once len(members) >= maxMembers (64, the W3C compliance
# number), the parse layer enforces the combined 8192 byte budget, per-member
# byte limits are dropped as non-spec, and empty results are reported
# through the error handler. No pool correction.
#
# ## What fires, both revisions
#
# Three --cache off runs per revision report identical output: scanning the
# whole repository gives 282 findings at the vulnerable parent and 283 at
# the fix, and the entire difference is a line-number shift inside
# _test.go files -- nothing on propagation/baggage.go or baggage/baggage.go
# in either revision, in the default report or under the flags view. The
# default report does carry go.flags@resolved rows (four
# code.UnboundedAllocationReview findings live on unrelated otest
# collectors), so this is a real zero and not a -flags-only misread.
# `vyql match` on both source files reports no source and no sink label at
# all: carrier.Values is unbound, so no taint route exists either.
#
# ## Why the near-miss analysis does not reach it
#
# The one relevant later-shipped item is goUnboundedAppendAccumulation
# (bindings/go/unboundedAppendAccumulation.vyql), which reports exactly this
# shape -- a loop growing a slice through x = append(x, ...) whose enclosing
# function never compares that slice's len or cap -- through the existing
# code.UnboundedAllocationReview concept and VYQL-DOS-041. Its frontend fact
# (golang.go goUnboundedAppendAccumulationObservations) only inspects
# *ast.ForStmt with a nil Cond, i.e. the C-style conditionless `for {}`
# decode loop; a `for _, x := range xs` body is a *ast.RangeStmt and
# produces no fact. A minimal pair confirms it: the same accumulation is
# labelled in the `for {}` spelling and unlabelled in the range spelling.
#
# ## Why widening it is measured unshippable
#
# Extending that one function to *ast.RangeStmt was probed on the real
# checkouts. Taking every range-loop accumulation reports 141
# UnboundedAllocationReview findings on this repository against a baseline
# of 4, and the additional 137 are ordinary mapping code -- exporters/otlp
# transform loops, bridge/opencensus converters, metricdatatest comparisons
# -- appending one constructed element per iteration. Narrowing to the
# principled form (a range loop runs exactly len(rangeExpr) times, so only
# a spread -- append(dst, src...) -- grows the output by an amount each
# iteration's data decides, whereas a conditionless for loop is unbounded
# even appending single elements) drops it to 17 and keeps a clean
# discrimination: propagation/baggage.go:64 is reported at the vulnerable
# parent and gone at the fix, whose `if len(members) >= maxMembers` is
# exactly the bound goComparesLenOrCap looks for. But the collateral stays
# decisive -- across six real Go trees the narrowed form adds 47 findings
# for this one true positive (opentelemetry-go +13, consul +14, neuvector
# +18, distribution +2, grafana and vitess +0), and the false positives are
# structurally identical to the hit: `for _, p := range producers {
# data = append(data, p.Read()...) }` differs from the vulnerable loop only
# in what is being ranged over. That discriminator -- whether the ranged
# collection is peer-controlled -- is source information the syntactic loop
# fact does not carry, and no binding labels carrier.Values, so keying on it
# means naming this SDK's own interface. The discriminator is not in the
# construct; the same cross-iteration budget family as ranks 1554 and 1560.
#
# Both blocks below reject VYQL-DOS-041: the unbounded accumulation and the
# capped form are equally unreported. When accumulation-budget vocabulary
# that is source-aware lands, the first block must flip to expect.

test "rank1534: members accumulate from every header without a budget"
  lang go
  file propagation/baggage.go
  reject VYQL-DOS-041
  code
  ```
  package propagation

  import (
      "context"

      "go.opentelemetry.io/otel/baggage"
  )

  const baggageHeader = "baggage"

  func extractMultiBaggage(parent context.Context, carrier ValuesGetter) context.Context {
      bVals := carrier.Values(baggageHeader)
      if len(bVals) == 0 {
          return parent
      }
      var members []baggage.Member
      for _, bStr := range bVals {
          currBag, err := baggage.Parse(bStr)
          if err != nil {
              continue
          }
          members = append(members, currBag.Members()...)
      }

      b, err := baggage.New(members...)
      if err != nil || b.Len() == 0 {
          return parent
      }
      return baggage.ContextWithBaggage(parent, b)
  }
  ```

test "rank1534: the fixed revision stops at the member budget"
  lang go
  file propagation/baggage.go
  reject VYQL-DOS-041
  code
  ```
  package propagation

  import (
      "context"

      "go.opentelemetry.io/otel/baggage"
      "go.opentelemetry.io/otel/internal/errorhandler"
  )

  const (
      baggageHeader = "baggage"

      // W3C Baggage specification limits.
      // https://www.w3.org/TR/baggage/#limits
      maxMembers = 64
  )

  func extractMultiBaggage(parent context.Context, carrier ValuesGetter) context.Context {
      bVals := carrier.Values(baggageHeader)
      if len(bVals) == 0 {
          return parent
      }

      var members []baggage.Member
      for _, bStr := range bVals {
          currBag, err := baggage.Parse(bStr)
          if err != nil {
              errorhandler.GetErrorHandler().Handle(err)
          }
          if currBag.Len() == 0 {
              continue
          }
          members = append(members, currBag.Members()...)
          if len(members) >= maxMembers {
              break
          }
      }

      b, err := baggage.New(members...)
      if err != nil {
          errorhandler.GetErrorHandler().Handle(err)
      }
      if b.Len() == 0 {
          return parent
      }
      return baggage.ContextWithBaggage(parent, b)
  }
  ```
